            <div xml:id="d318">

                <pb facs="0551" n="437" sameAs="frus:frus1931v01/pg437"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A15a3/1660: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Dawes</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-11-12T16:00:00+00:00">November 12, 1931—4
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received November 12—1:07 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>435. Craigie informs me by informal letter that Massigli in Paris today received
                    the proposals to which reference was made in my telegram 414, October 13, 3
                        p.m.<anchor n="39" xml:id="d318-f1"/> He outlined orally how far the French
                    point of view was met by these proposals in that the program rather than being
                    spread over 6 years, which was according to the British interpretation of the
                    Bases of Agreement, now refers to a 5-year period. Three thousand tons of
                    construction by France and Italy respectively, which is not permitted before
                    1936 by the London Naval Treaty, is excluded from this 5-year building
                    program.</p>
                <p>Brussels is receiving cipher text.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dawes</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
